Normalization is done in numpy (pyloudnorm gain + look-ahead true-peak limiter) instead of ffmpeg loudnorm: the source is mastered to +0.44 dBTP, so a -2.2 dB linear gain still exceeded TP=-1.5 and loudnorm silently switched the whole mix to dynamic mode (compressing the background everywhere, violating 'untouched'); widening LRA did not help since the trigger was true peak. loudnorm also resamples to 192 kHz and returned 15 extra samples. The limiter only touches samples whose 4x-oversampled peak exceeds the limit (min-filter then moving average keeps gain <= what each peak needs). Ducking uses a deterministic gain envelope rather than sidechaincompress so no-speech sections are bit-exact x one gain. MIX_KEEP_VOCALS_DB defaults off because the vocals stem outside segments contained untranscribed Japanese speech (30.5 s, 36 s).
